\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



The lower cutoff on $k$ is chosen using $\epsilon$ alone, before the infinitely many endpoints $n$ are selected. In particular, it cannot be moved close to $n$ to discard bad values. The second question asks about failure of a stronger uniform bound.

For $n=\prod_pp^{v_p(n)}$, $\omega(n)=\#\{p:v_p(n)>0\}$ counts distinct prime factors, whereas $\Omega(n)=\sum_pv_p(n)$ would count with multiplicity. The displayed question uses the former. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://www.erdosproblems.com/679}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $\epsilon>0$ and $\omega(n)$ count the number of distinct prime factors of $n$. Are there infinitely many values of $n$ such that $ \omega(n-k) < (1+\epsilon)\frac{\log k}{\log\log k} $ for all $k<n$ which are sufficiently large depending on $\epsilon$ only?
Can one show the stronger version with $ \omega(n-k) < \frac{\log k}{\log\log k}+O(1) $ is false? \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Residual task reported by the archive.}

The embedded review (2026-08-17) records: Prove the original (1+epsilon)log k/log log k simultaneous upper bound for infinitely many n or establish Lau's predicted logarithmic obstruction. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Are there infinitely many endpoints behind which every sufficiently distant integer has no more than the prescribed near-logarithmic number of prime factors?

